      "risk_7d": "10% LOW",
      "next_update": null,
      "raw_text": "Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast:\nA large non-tropical low pressure area centered a few hundred miles \neast of northeastern Florida continues to produce disorganized \nshowers and thunderstorms. The system is moving into an \nenvironment that will probably not be conducive for development of \nsignificant tropical or subtropical characteristics. \n\nAlthough the chances of tropical development continue to decrease, \nthis large weather system is still expected to produce gusty winds, \ndangerous surf, rip currents, and coastal flooding along portions \nof the southeast and mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week. \nIn addition, heavy rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will \nmove northward over coastal portions of the Carolinas and the \nmid-Atlantic through the weekend. Interests along the U.S. \nEast Coast should continue to monitor the progress of this system. \nAdditional information on this system, including Gale Warnings, can \nbe found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather \nService, as well as products issued by your local National Weather \nService forecast office.\n* Formation chance through 48 hours...low...10 percent.\n* Formation chance through 7 days...low...10 percent.",
      "metadata": {
        "chance_48h_level": "LOW",
